# Cloudflare Pages reads this file and does not serve it.
#
# The bundle and the stylesheet have fixed names - the build always writes
# public/assets/app.js - so a browser holding a cached copy keeps running the
# old app after a deploy. `no-cache` keeps the copy but revalidates it before
# every use. The ETag does the real work: an unchanged file answers 304 with
# no body, so this costs one small conditional request per load rather than
# re-downloading 186KB.
#
# What actually caused the four-hour staleness was not Pages. The zone in
# front of mj.spyrostrimis.com had Browser Cache TTL set to 4 hours, and that
# REWROTE the header below to max-age=14400 on the custom domain while
# mj-4er.pages.dev served the no-cache this file asks for - same deployment,
# two different headers, and the file looked like it was being ignored. The
# setting is now "Respect Existing Headers" (changed 2026-09-21). If a deploy
# ever seems to take hours to reach a browser again, check that before this
# file, and compare the two hostnames to tell them apart.
#
# Pages' own default for a static asset is `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`
# - not the four hours the zone was imposing. So anything without a rule here
# is revalidated on every load.
#
# Fonts are the opposite case from the bundle: ten woff2 files that never
# change under a given name, and the largest thing the app serves. They are
# pinned for a year and never revalidated, which costs nothing per load and
# makes one demand in return - a refreshed font MUST be given a new filename,
# or browsers keep the old one for a year. See fonts/README.md.
#
# /trips/ photos are deliberately left revalidating. They can be replaced
# under an existing filename, and they are looked at rarely, so a 304 per view
# is the cheaper mistake.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/css/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
